@id("close-workorder-main-with-recent-person-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Tolvick___get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    output.decision: Drupe::decision::"ACCEPTED",
    output.approver_kind: Drupe::approver_kind::"PERSON"
  }
};

// The approval lookup must itself be permitted, or it is never recorded as a response.
@id("allow-tolvick-approval-lookup")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Tolvick___get_approval", resource);

// The agent can never approve work orders.
@id("forbid-agent-approval")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___approve_workorder", resource);
```

I used only Tolvick for the approval check, since it is where approvals are decided and recorded. `Dorvane___get_approval` is not permitted. The `Drupe::decision::"ACCEPTED"` and `Drupe::approver_kind::"PERSON"` enum literals are my reading of how a temporal pattern matches enum-typed output fields; I have not validated them against the engine.

I also treated `Tolvick___get_approval` as the only read-only lookup needed, so other read tools such as `get_workorder` stay denied. If you want those allowed too, each needs its own plain `permit`.

